.\"
.\" Describes a driver written from a functional specification of the
.\" Raspberry Pi firmware clock interface.
.\"
.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt RPI_FW_CLK 4 aarch64
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rpi_fw_clk
.Nd Raspberry Pi firmware clocks
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in the
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options FDT"
.Cd "device clk"
.Cd "device rpi_fw_clk"
.Ed
.Pp
To load the driver as a module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload rpi_fw_clk
.Ed
.Pp
The driver needs the Raspberry Pi firmware mailbox driver,
.Li rpi_fw .
A module loaded after the system has started does not get its device by
itself; see
.Sx Attaching .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On the Raspberry Pi the VideoCore firmware owns the clocks of the system on
a chip.
It changes them by itself, to protect the chip against overheating and
under-voltage, so they are read and set only by asking the firmware, never
by programming the clock hardware.
The
.Nm
driver is the clock provider for the clocks the firmware offers.
A consumer's device tree node names one by the firmware's clock id.
.Pp
At attach the driver asks the firmware which clocks exist.
Of those it makes a clock for each of
.Li arm ,
.Li core ,
.Li v3d ,
.Li isp ,
.Li pixel ,
.Li hevc ,
.Li m2mc ,
.Li pixel_bvb ,
.Li vec
and
.Li disp ,
called
.Li rpi_fw_ Ns Ar name .
The firmware's other clocks
.Po
.Li emmc ,
.Li uart ,
.Li h264 ,
.Li sdram ,
.Li pwm
and
.Li emmc2
.Pc
are shown by the driver's sysctl variables and are not offered to
consumers.
The clocks have no parents: the parent the firmware reports for each is
shown and not used.
.Pp
The driver behaves as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The lowest and the highest rate the firmware allows a clock are read once,
at attach, and bound the rates the driver sets.
.It
Enabling or disabling a clock sends nothing to the firmware.
Whether a clock is running is what the firmware says.
.It
Every read of a rate asks the firmware.
A read that fails gives 0 Hz.
.It
The driver does not round rates.
The firmware does, and the rate it applied is read back from it.
.It
A new rate is sent to the firmware only if it differs from the rate the
clock has.
.El
.Pp
A request for a rate is treated in one of two ways.
For
.Li arm
the requested rate is brought into the clock's range and set.
Every other clock is kept as low as its consumer allows: the requested
rate is taken as the least the consumer needs, the clock is set to the
larger of it and the lowest rate of the clock's range, and a request above
the highest rate of the range fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The clock
.Li m2mc
has a floor of 120 MHz: with that clock stopped, an access to the HDMI
hardware it drives locks the bus.
The lowest rate of its range is the larger of the floor and the lowest
rate the firmware allows, but not more than the highest the firmware
allows.
If the clock is below that rate when the driver attaches it is set to it at
once, before any consumer can reach it, and the driver sets it to no less
afterwards.
.Pp
The clock framework keeps the last rate it has seen for a clock, and the
firmware changes clocks without telling anyone.
A kernel consumer that needs the rate or the state of a clock as it is now
calls
.Fn rpi_fw_clk_query ,
and
.Fn rpi_fw_clk_max_rate
for the highest rate the firmware allows; both are declared in
.In arm64/broadcom/bcm2712/rpi_fw_clk.h .
.Ss Attaching
A generic driver takes every device tree node named
.Dq clocks .
Compiled into the kernel,
.Nm
takes the firmware's node ahead of it.
If the firmware mailbox has not attached by then, the driver keeps the
node and asks the firmware again at each later pass over the bus, and a
last time when interrupts are enabled.
.Pp
Loaded as a module after the system has started, the driver finds the node
already attached to the generic driver.
The node is then moved with
.Xr devctl 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
devctl set driver -f ofw_clkbusN rpi_fw_clk
.Ed
.Pp
Once its clocks exist the driver cannot be detached, and the module cannot
be unloaded: a clock cannot be taken away from the clock framework.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports the firmware's clock node, a child of the firmware node,
with the compatible string:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
raspberrypi,firmware-clocks
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following read-only variables are available under
.Va dev.rpi_fw_clk.%d .
There is a group
.Va %s
for each clock the firmware lists, by its name.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va status
How far the driver is:
.Dq waiting for the firmware mailbox ,
.Dq registered
or
.Dq failed .
.It Va %s.id
The firmware's id of the clock.
.It Va %s.parent
The id of the parent the firmware reports for it.
.It Va %s.fw_min , %s.fw_max
The lowest and the highest rate the firmware allows, in Hz, as read at
attach.
Reading one fails if the firmware refused to give it.
.It Va %s.rate
The clock's rate in Hz, asked of the firmware when the variable is read.
.It Va %s.state
The clock's state word, asked of the firmware when the variable is read.
Bit 0 is set if the clock is running, bit 1 if there is no such clock.
.It Va %s.lower , %s.upper
The range into which the driver brings the rates it sets, in Hz.
Present for the clocks offered to consumers.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: waiting for the firmware mailbox"
The mailbox driver has not attached yet; the driver will ask again.
Printed in a verbose boot.
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: the firmware mailbox did not attach; no clocks"
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: the firmware mailbox is not attached"
There is no mailbox driver to ask the firmware through.
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: cannot list the clocks: error %d"
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: firmware lists clock id %u, above %u"
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: cannot read the range of clock %s: error %d"
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: cannot register the clocks: error %d"
The firmware's answers could not be used.
There are no clocks.
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: clock %s: the firmware maximum, %u Hz, is below the floor of %u Hz; the maximum is used"
The firmware allows the clock no rate as high as its floor.
The lowest rate of the clock's range is then the highest the firmware
allows.
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: cannot raise clock %s to %u Hz: error %d"
The firmware did not accept the least rate the driver sets for the clock.
There are no clocks.
.It "rpi_fw_clk%d: cannot set clock %s to %ju Hz: error %d"
The firmware did not accept a rate a consumer asked for.
Printed at most once a second.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fdt 4 ,
.Xr v3d 4 ,
.Xr devctl 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The rate the clock framework reports for one of these clocks is the one it
saw last, which need not be the clock's rate now.
